  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{x}^{2}).$ Given that ${f}^{\u2032}(0)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{\u2033}(0)>0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=0$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $x=0$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+{x}^{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2033}(x)=\\frac{2-2{x}^{2}}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2033}(0) & =\\frac{2-2(0)^{2}}{{(1+{0}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
